<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christine de Pizan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1364">1364</a>–c. 1430</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Christine de Pisan, Cristina da Pizzano</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christine de Pizan was a Venetian-born French court writer in prose and poetry for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a>. Born in the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in 1364, she came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as a child when her father became court astrologer to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>. After her husband died in 1389, she supported her mother and children by writing and has been called the first professional woman of letters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Her works, among the earliest feminist writings, include The Book of the City of Ladies and The Treasure of the City of Ladies, written while she worked for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-fearless">John the Fearless</a>. In 1429 she published the Ditié de Jehanne d'Arc, celebrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joan-of-arc">Joan of Arc</a>'s victory over the English; she died around 1430.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christine-de-pizan.jpg" alt="Christine de Pizan" width="330" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: photography The British Library Board, Harley 4431, f.259v · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christine_de_Pisan_-_cathedra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
